\newlabel{fig:14.01}{{14.2}{95}{}{}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {14.1}{\ignorespaces Schematic representation of the SIR model compartmental structure and transitions. Susceptible individuals (compartment $S$) become infected at a rate proportional to contacts with infectious individuals in compartment $I$. Infected individuals recover at rate $\gamma $ and enter the recovered compartment $R$ with lasting immunity. All compartments face a baseline death rate $\mu $. Additionally, infectious individuals face an extra disease-induced death rate $\delta $ owing to pathogenic effects.}}{95}{}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\numberline {14.3}Normalizing the Model}{95}{}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\numberline {14.4}Fixed Points and Stability Analysis}{96}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:14.02}{{14.4}{98}{}{}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {14.2}{\ignorespaces Steady-state values and stability domains as a function of the basic reproduction number, $R_0$. (Left) In the disease-free state, the fraction of susceptible individuals, $s^*$, remains at 1, represented by the blue curve. However, in the endemic-disease state $s^*$ decreases inversely with $R_0$, shown by the red curve. (Right) The normalized infected fraction at equilibrium, $i^*$, is zero in the disease-free state (blue curve) and increases as $1 - 1/R_0$ in the endemic-disease state (red curve). A transcritical bifurcation occurs at R0=1. For R0 < 1, the disease-free equilibrium is stable (solid lines) while the endemic steady state is unstable (dashed lines). At the bifurcation point, the two equilibria collide and their stability reverses for R0 > 1.}}{98}{}\protected@file@percent }
